# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe CAD relative CLI load path resolves inside the workdir
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-cad-relative-cli-load-path

* Symptom

On AutoCAD 2022, this command completed successfully but printed none of the
probe's 240 observations:

#+begin_example
alfe --keep-workdir --verbose --debug --mode batch -norc --cad autocad \
     -l ./autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/language/comparison-operators-probe.lsp
#+end_example

The same source printed normally when loaded directly in the AutoCAD console,
and alfe printed it normally under BricsCAD and clautolisp.

* Cause

The CAD backend forwarded the relative =-l= spelling unchanged.  AutoCAD's
=accoreconsole= process runs from alfe's generated workdir, not from alfe's
invocation directory, so it searched for the source below the workdir and did
not find it.  On AutoCAD's no-=(&rest)= runtime path the load normaliser pads
the call with a =nil= on-failure value, which made that missing file look like
a successful =DONE 1 OK= request with empty stdout and stderr.

Passing the same file as an absolute pathname immediately produced all 240
observations, proving that output capture was functioning and distinguishing
this incident from =autocad-no-rest-output-capture.issue=.

* Resolved (2026-09-25, alfe 2.2.84)

=stage-source-as-utf8-bom= now resolves every CAD =-l= source against alfe's
live invocation directory before either passing it through or transcoding it.
Thus both AutoCAD and BricsCAD receive an absolute pathname, while the command
line retains its normal caller-relative meaning.

The regression test
=cad-cli-load-path-is-made-absolute-before-crossing-process-boundary= first
failed on the unchanged relative string and now pins the absolute pathname.
The exact reported command was rerun against real AutoCAD 2022 after rebuilding
alfe and produced =OBSERVATIONS 240=.
